# Mechanism: from-url — HTTPS release/asset → declared path.
# Format: <dest>  <url>  sha256=<64-hex>  [requires=java,...]
# <dest> is repo-root-relative unless it starts with ~ or /.
#
# sha256= is MANDATORY on every row and the realizer refuses a row
# without one (matching from-elan and from-autotools-tarball). A URL is
# a name, not a pin: GitHub release assets can be re-uploaded under an
# unchanged tag, so only the digest says which bytes you got.
#
# Before adding a row, answer both: (1) what reads this path, and
# (2) what digest pins these bytes. A fetch that fails either question
# is attack surface, not a dependency.
#
# ── Alloy (081M23AST90087G0R00150MK76) ──────────────────────────────
#
# READS IT: tests/Tests.FSharp/Formal/Alloy.Runner.Tests.fs and
# src/Core.TypeScript/formal-verification/run-alloy.ts, both by the
# literal path src/Core.Alloy/alloy.jar.
#
# PINS IT: the sha256 below. MEASURED 2026-09-09 -- the jar that was
# committed to git at src/Core.Alloy/alloy.jar and the upstream asset
# org.alloytools.alloy.dist.jar of release v6.2.0 are BYTE-IDENTICAL
# (both sha256 6b8c1cb5...), so de-vendoring changed the verifier by
# exactly nothing. v6.2.0 (published 2025-01-09) is also the LATEST
# Alloy release: `gh api repos/AlloyTools/org.alloytools.alloy/releases`
# lists nothing newer, and releases/latest returns v6.2.0. The tag
# carries no rolling hazard either -- every asset on it has
# created_at == updated_at == 2025-01-09T08:18, untouched since.
src/Core.Alloy/alloy.jar  https://github.com/AlloyTools/org.alloytools.alloy/releases/download/v6.2.0/org.alloytools.alloy.dist.jar  sha256=6b8c1cb5bc93bedfc7c61435c4e1ab6e688a242dc702a394628d9a9801edb78d
#
# ── ROLLING ROWS: rolling=<tag> ─────────────────────────────────
#
# Most upstreams publish an IMMUTABLE asset: the tag is cut once, the
# bytes never move, and the sha256 below is a formality that only ever
# fires on corruption or attack. Some do not. A row may carry
#
#   rolling=<the mutable upstream name>
#   identity=<jar-tlc|jar-alloy>            how to derive a human identity
#   remeasure=<script>[:<arg>...]           the evidence run, argv-encoded
#   pinsurfaces=<path>[,<path>...]          every OTHER file carrying this
#                                           digest or identity
#   derivedpins=<path>[,<path>...]          files whose OWN sha256 is
#                                           recorded inside a pinsurface
#
# to declare that the bytes behind the URL are REPLACED IN PLACE. The
# digest stays mandatory and stays enforced -- a rolling row fails
# CLOSED on a rebuild exactly like any other, and nothing auto-accepts.
# What `rolling=` buys is the DIAGNOSIS: the realizer stops saying
# "sha256 mismatch" (which reads as corruption) and says "upstream
# rebuilt this asset in place", prints both digests, and names the one
# command that re-measures and re-pins.
#
# RE-PINNING IS `bun tools/setup/repin-rolling.ts <dest>`, AND IT RUNS
# THE RE-MEASURE ITSELF. Bumping the digest by hand to make a red tree
# green is THE failure mode this whole regime exists to prevent: it
# would silently swap the verifier under every claim the verifier ever
# established. The tool refuses to write a digest whose `remeasure=`
# run did not pass, and `lint-verifier-jar-provenance.ts` refuses a
# rolling pin with no matching row in `from-url-rolling-receipts`.
#
# ── TLA+ / tla2tools (081M23ESC5B087G0R002HJ39DG) ─────────────────
#
# READS IT: registry/tlc-models.json `toolchain.jar`, which is the only
# source of the path for both runners -- run-tlc.ts and the xUnit gate
# tests/Tests.FSharp/Formal/Tlc.Runner.Tests.fs.
#
# NOT A PIN SURFACE, deliberately: nci-witness-receipt.ts stays pinned to
# TLC2 2026.05.18.174321. That receipt is a dated claim about which
# checker produced a witness, not a statement about which checker we run
# now, so advancing it here would have republished a 2026-09-06 research
# artefact to match a newer build. Its test reads those exact bytes back
# out of git by blob id instead.
#
# PINS IT: the sha256 below. MEASURED 2026-09-09.
#
# WHY THE ROLLING TAG AND NOT THE IMMUTABLE v1.7.4: because a newer
# model checker finds cases an older one misses, and a forced
# re-measure is the point rather than the cost (maintainer, 2026-09-09:
# "the updates may find new cases"). v1.7.4 is immutable and passes
# only 51/52 gate models -- it prints the generic "Temporal properties
# were violated." where 1.8.0 names WHICH property, so the
# QuorumCollateralDeterrenceR2 witness loses the discrimination the
# registry pins as expectDetail. An immutable verifier that cannot tell
# one violated property from another is the weaker artefact.
#
# WHAT THE ROLLING TAG COSTS, MEASURED RATHER THAN GUESSED: tlaplus
# republishes this asset from its CI, and its CI runs on pushes to
# master, which is bursty. Over the 180 days to 2026-09-09 master had
# commits on 24 distinct days -- but on 2026-09-09 the asset was
# rebuilt THREE times in four hours:
#
#   12:48:04  rev 65fbace
#   15:25:40  rev 4ad12e8   sha256 82cc5759...  52/52
#   16:25:36  rev 4ad12e8   sha256 bb82311b...  52/52   <- pinned below
#
# Note the last two share a rev: same source, different bytes, and no
# new master commit between them -- consistent with several matrix legs
# of one long CI run each uploading the artefact. So a rebuild is often
# not a new checker at all, and the digest cannot tell the difference,
# which is exactly why the re-measure is mechanical rather than a
# judgement call.
#
# Expect this pin to fail closed on the order of weekly, with several
# moves inside one upstream CI burst. THE OPEN QUESTION THAT COST
# BUYS, recorded for the maintainer rather than decided here: mirroring
# the measured bytes to one immutable asset under our own org would
# keep every property asked for (no jar in git, newest checker, clone
# at a tag still sufficient) and remove the fleet-wide breakage window
# -- at the price of becoming a redistributor of an MIT-licensed
# third-party binary. See 081M23ESC5B087G0R002HJ39DG.
src/Core.TLA/tla2tools.jar  https://github.com/tlaplus/tlaplus/releases/download/v1.8.0/tla2tools.jar  sha256=8836549e83db7f0b3f9fdde679ab56270d18e06198366d217d960738c02b9dbe  requires=java  rolling=tlaplus-v1.8.0-prerelease  update=when-drift-bump-pin  identity=jar-tlc  remeasure=src/Core.TypeScript/formal-verification/run-tlc.ts:--all  pinsurfaces=registry/tlc-models.json,docs/INSTALLED.md,docs/dependency-status.md
